    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "hey, im looking into some stuff for a project in london. we're comparing signalized intersections vs roundabouts for a major junction. can you explain the main differences in terms of traffic throughput and how they handle peak hour flow?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Signalized intersections typically offer higher peak hour throughput due to their ability to strictly filter and delay traffic volumes, preventing gridlock even if one lane is congested. Roundabouts, while generally better for managing steady-state flow and pedestrian safety, usually achieve higher capacity only when all approaches have strong traffic volumes present simultaneously; otherwise, they can become bottlenecks. Consequently, for a major junction in London characterized by highly variable traffic patterns during rush hour, signals often provide the necessary reliability to maintain flow, whereas roundabouts may require significant geometric optimization to match that capacity.",
