    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "that makes sense. one thing we're worried about is the \"friction\" in the system—like when things just stop or get weirdly congested for no obvious reason even when volume is okay. how do both designs handle those kinds of sudden disruptions or unexpected drops in flow?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Signalized intersections often suffer from pulsing effects or \"phantom jams\" caused by unsafe/inefficient spacing between successive platoons, leading to sudden stop-and-go waves that paradoxically increase congestion. In contrast, roundabouts are inherently more robust against these specific wave formations, but they can experience secondary friction like circulating queue back-ups if optimal design angles or junction layouts are not strictly followed. For a project in London, roundabouts typically offer better resilience against these sudden disruptions unless the volume is extremely high, while signals depend heavily on precise actuator timing to avoid creating new, unnecessary friction during minor flow variations.",
      "source": "target",
